The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
if (!xe)
    return;
...
xe_vdev->vfid = pci_iov_vf_id(pdev) + 1;
xe_vdev->xe = xe;
```
```
xe_vfio_pci_migration_init(xe_vdev);
```
Reason: `xe_vfio_pci_migration_init()` contained the only assignments to `xe_vdev->xe` and `xe_vdev->vfid`, but it returned early without setting them when migration was not supported. Since `xe_vfio_pci_init_dev()` called only this function to initialize those fields, VF devices without migration left them as NULL/0. Later, reset operations like `xe_sriov_vfio_wait_flr_done()` dereferenced the uninitialized `xe_vdev->xe`, causing a page fault.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
static int xe_vfio_pci_vf_init(struct xe_vfio_pci_core_device *xe_vdev)
{
    ...
    if (!pdev->is_virtfn)
        return 0;
    if (!xe)
        return -ENODEV;
    xe_vdev->xe = xe;
    xe_vdev->vfid = pci_iov_vf_id(pdev) + 1;
    xe_vfio_pci_migration_init(xe_vdev);
    return 0;
}
```
```
mutex_init(&xe_vdev->state_mutex);
spin_lock_init(&xe_vdev->reset_lock);
ret = xe_vfio_pci_vf_init(xe_vdev);
```
Reason: The new `xe_vfio_pci_vf_init()` function unconditionally sets `xe_vdev->xe` and `xe_vdev->vfid` for all VF devices (if the PF is available), decoupling this initialization from migration support. It is called from `xe_vfio_pci_init_dev()` after the mutex and spinlock are initialized globally. The migration init then only checks the migration capability using the now-valid `xe_vdev->xe` pointer, so no code paths leave the critical fields uninitialized.

